---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const title = 'Compare Shopify SMS marketing apps by workflow and cost';
const desc = 'Choose a Shopify SMS platform using a practical workflow trial, message cost worksheet, and migration checklist.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path="/">
  <section class="container py-16">
    <span class="badge">Shopify SMS buying guide</span>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Choose an SMS app around the campaign you need to run.</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-xl">Start with a specific audience, a useful message, and an owner for replies. Then compare the tools that can deliver that workflow within your budget. A cart reminder, a limited release, and a replenishment prompt need different data and timing.</p>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps/postscript">Evaluate Postscript</a><a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/pricing">Plan message costs</a></div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16 prose-shopify">
    <h2>Four candidates, four questions to take into a demo</h2>
    <p>These are workflow starting points, without a numerical ranking. The linked vendor listings were checked on November 25, 2026. They describe advertised capabilities; the trial exercises below are proposed checks, not results from merchant accounts.</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Candidate</th><th>Published scope</th><th>Decision question</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a></td><td>Shopify-oriented messaging campaigns and automations using historical store data.</td><td>Can your operator build and maintain a launch audience without repeated exports?</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a></td><td>Email and SMS, signup units, segmentation, and expert strategy support.</td><td>Does the proposed service scope solve a staffing or acquisition problem you actually have?</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a></td><td>Email and SMS campaigns with segmentation and automated workflows.</td><td>Can the team coordinate both channels using the customer data already in its account?</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a></td><td>Email and SMS with signup forms and welcome and cart automations.</td><td>Can one operator maintain the capture-to-first-purchase journey at the planned list size?</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>Primary sources: <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/postscript-sms-marketing">Postscript listing</a>, <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/attentive">Attentive listing</a>, <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/klaviyo-email-marketing">Klaviyo listing</a>, and <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/omnisend">Omnisend listing</a>. Feature availability, destinations, and account terms need to match your particular store.</p>
    <p>If your email program already works, compare the work of adding SMS to that platform with the work of maintaining a separate sender. A separate tool adds another place to manage events, permissions, and campaign scheduling. Its benefit must outweigh that work. For Sequenzy or another platform, verify the required Shopify event integration, account channel availability, and cross-channel suppression before relying on shared customer data.</p>
    <h2>Write a campaign brief before you compare interfaces</h2>
    <p>For each planned send, record the audience, the reason to interrupt, the destination page, the offer, the exclusions, and the outcome to measure. A waitlisted shopper needs the correct variant link. A recent buyer needs to leave a recovery queue. A subscription customer may need a renewal reminder instead of a competing clearance offer.</p>
    <p>Choose one bottleneck. If you cannot identify interested shoppers, test the data and segmentation first. If your list is small, inspect capture and the signup promise. If sends already work but costs are rising, compare message billing and incremental profit before paying for another feature bundle.</p>
    <h2>A limited-release trial you can reproduce</h2>
    <p>Use a test product and test contacts. Give contacts different preferences and purchase states. Create an audience for that product, exclude people who already bought it, and preview the resulting membership. Change one contact's state and check when the audience updates. Record the delay rather than assuming that an integration is instant.</p>
    <p>Draft an access message linked to the actual variant. Check that the landing page works on a phone, the stock statement is true, the offer is clear, and the opt-out path works. Assign someone to handle replies. Schedule a related email only if it adds information, and inspect the combined calendar for duplicate offers.</p>
    <p>For a later live pilot, define a stop rule before sending: an agreed budget, reply workload, or opt-out threshold. A smaller eligible audience may be appropriate, but there is no universal monthly send cap that fits every signup promise and buying cycle. Start with the frequency you can justify and evaluate it against customer response.</p>
    <h2>Compare the peak month, not just the entry plan</h2>
    <p>List planned broadcasts and triggered messages separately. For each destination, estimate eligible recipients, billable message segments, SMS versus MMS mix, and the applicable rate. Then add subscription fees, carrier charges, minimum spend, and paid services. Ask whether a quoted minimum includes usage or is charged on top of it.</p>
    <p>Illustrative arithmetic: 2,000 recipients receiving two one-segment messages create 4,000 billable message units. At an assumed $0.01 per unit, the messaging line is $40 before other charges. These are worksheet assumptions, not a vendor quote. Longer copy, character encoding, and destination rules can change the billable units.</p>
    <p>Judge a promotion on contribution after product cost, discounts, fulfillment, returns, and messaging. Platform-attributed sales alone do not establish incremental sales. Where sample size permits, keep a randomly selected eligible group unsent and compare outcomes over the same window. Do not interpret a small or uneven pilot as proof of lift.</p>
    <h2>Keep permission and purchase events intact</h2>
    <p>Before importing numbers, preserve the original opt-in source, timestamp, disclosure, and suppression history. Email permission does not establish SMS permission. Confirm destination eligibility and sending restrictions with current provider guidance and the requirements applicable to your audience; a single time window is not a complete policy for every location.</p>
    <p>Test an unsubscribe, a purchase made while a reminder is queued, and a delayed event. Give one person responsibility for resolving discrepancies between systems. When moving providers, pause overlapping sends and reconcile active audiences before resuming. A successful CSV import is only one part of a migration.</p>
    <h2>A month of implementation with clear outputs</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Week one:</strong> inventory capture points, consent records, current sends, and campaign owners. Resolve missing records before expanding the audience.</li>
      <li><strong>Week two:</strong> build one welcome or recovery workflow. Test eligibility, purchase exits, links, replies, and billing estimates.</li>
      <li><strong>Week three:</strong> add one catalog-specific journey: a genuine restock, a refill reminder based on expected use, or a release for interested shoppers.</li>
      <li><strong>Week four:</strong> review cost, opt-outs, support load, and contribution. Remove or revise a weak send before adding another.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>Choose the next page by the unresolved decision</h2>
    <p>Use the <a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript evaluation</a> for a Shopify messaging trial. Compare the nearby alternative in <a href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a>, or examine <a href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a> when one team wants both channels. The <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> addresses spend, while <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> addresses conflicting sends.</p>
    <p>For catalog planning, start with <a href="/use-cases/product-launch">product launches</a>, <a href="/use-cases/replenishment">replenishment</a>, or <a href="/for">store types</a>. Use those pages to frame questions for your own implementation rather than treating an example as a guaranteed outcome.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
